Z||000005||batboy||03-07-2000||12:04 AM||||The RCC order was $515 plus shipping (forgot how much), the Moog ball joints were about $50 each and the tie rods were about $30 a piece. The rear control arm Prothane poly bushing kit was $30 plus shipping. I had to order a half shaft axle assembly too (have a bad CV joint) and I have a new rear sway bar with poly end links on the way. All total, I'll have close to $1,000 US into the rear end of the Batmobile by the time I'm done.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||03-07-2000||06:44 PM||||Just a thought,but have you considered reinforcing the control arms while they're out? A couple pieces of 1/8X1-1/4 flat bar welded diagonally across the underside would help,cheap too.<BR>Those bushings will make the shifts alot more crisp with that V8 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||03-07-2000||10:37 PM||||LowCG, I hadn't thought of doing that until you mentioned it. Right now is the ideal time if I were to do it. The old ball joints are off and the old bushings are out. New parts ready to go on. The control arms should be bare before any welding is done.<P>Better add another $150 to that earlier total for the rear suspension. I decided to replace both of the drive axles instead of just one. I also ordered a pair of those fancy expensive combination axle bearing and seal from the Fiero Store. I'm going whole hog here. No more low budget.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||03-08-2000||06:37 PM||||how does their bump steer mount? take one more pic with a new tie rod and an old one together please. I'd like to see what the diferance is. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Richard Parnell||03-08-2000||07:26 PM||||Batboy- What you're doing is what I've really been wanting to do to mr car in prep for the V8. (I'm drooling here)  If it's not asking too much, could you put together a rundown on what steps you went through and what problems you encountered in the process after you're done?||||reg||5
Z||000010||william_thorniii||03-08-2000||10:55 PM||||Bat,<P>You're my He-ro (reminiscent of Ferris Bueller)...<P>In fact you inspired me so much, I just spent quite a large sum of $ on some Belltech drop spindles and an 88 cradle with all necessary links and the Ryane strut relocation kit.  The only thing that I need now is the coil overs.  I am sure you did your RCC/Ryanne homework, so fill me in... how do they stack up?<P>Many Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000011||batboy||03-08-2000||11:55 PM||||LowCG: I took your advice, my rear control arms are in the welding shop getting some reinforcing 1/8 inch flat stock welded onto the back side for added strength.<P>Ogre: I scanned in a diagram from the instructions, maybe this will help you figure it out.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/diagram.jpg"> <P>Richard: I haven't been keeping detailed notes on this project like I should of, but I've been trying to document every step of the Batmobiles transformation here in the Forum, probably to the point that some people are tired of hearing about my hunk of junk. When I complete the suspension mods and get it back on the road, I'll write up a little summary. In the mean time, you can follow my current work on a daily basis for the month of March in another topic in the general forum called March Batmobile Updates.<P>WT3: Sound sweet! You must be burning through as much or more $$$ as me. I don't think you can go wrong with coil-over kits from either RCC or Ryane. Actually there are three primary choices, I believe Mike is trying Shelby's coil-over kit, which is cheaper, but I know nothing about it. It'll be interesting to learn what he finds out and whether he likes his setup.<P> <A HREF="http://www.shelbycustom.com/r-coil/r-coil.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.shelbycustom.com/r-coil/r-coil.html</A> <P><BR>I chose the RCC coil-over conversion because in's a complete bolt in that does not require any additional modification and comes with a Gabriel Gas Ryder strut cartridge already installed.<P>The Ryane kit is cheaper, but you must buy your own strut and grind off the mounting hat. This gives you the flexibility of using different struts if you want. Ryane also offers their kits with Bilstein or Koni struts (very expensive though).||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||03-09-2000||11:18 AM||||They did what I figured they where doing. Thanks. What did just those parts cost? Is that inner shperical joint all metal or a lined unit. If it's an all metal unit you'll need to make sure it stays clean and lubricated. (There are teflon lined ones. you can usually see the edges of the liner. The lined ones are better for this since they don't need any regular maintinance beyond keeping them from getting caked with mud or something.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||03-09-2000||09:50 PM||||Ogre, the bump steer kit alone is $120 plus $10 shipping. I looked at the spherical joints and they are lined with a black material, don't know if it's nylon or teflon though.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||03-09-2000||10:31 PM||||As long as they are lined is what matters most. Just keep the mud off them and give'm a shake to make sure they arn't wearing once in a while.||||reg||1
Z||000015||FIEROCIOUS||03-10-2000||01:19 AM||||I'll keep everyone posted on the shelby cutoms coilovers. I've written them about their recomendations on spring rate with the Eibach front springs but have not (yet) recieved a responce I'll give them more time, but I REALY don't like being ignored!<BR>Has anyone else delt with them before?<P><BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000016||william_thorniii||03-10-2000||10:22 AM||||Mike,<P>I know that your set up will be slightly different to the reference one, but check out Ryane Motorsports.  If you go into the Slalom Front Suspension, you will find a chart listing three different reccomended spring rates for 3 or 4 different styles of driving.  This should give you a ball park # if you have an 84-87. If you have an 88, It should give you a # better than decent.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000017||86_Fastback||03-10-2000||04:02 PM||||I have Shelby coil-overs coming to go with the front Eibachs I picked up.  Shelby recommended 350# springs for Eibachs and Suspension Technique springs.  I will take pictures when they arrive and post em for every one to see.  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||FIEROCIOUS||03-10-2000||10:37 PM||||Thanks William ThornIII, I'll take a look at it! Thanxs for the info Big Willy style!||||reg||1
Z||000020||FIEROCIOUS||03-10-2000||11:29 PM||||OK, Now I REALY don't know what spring rate!<P>86_Fastback said Shelby customs recomends 350lb springs for the rear while the chart on ryanemotorsports is as follows:<P>Rear: 250 275 300 325 350 400 450 475 500<BR>Frnt: 225 250 275 300 325 375 400 425 550<P>By this chart they recomend 300lb springs according to my calculations in the thread "Spring Rates Again" the Eibachs are 275lb springs.<P>Any handling gurus out there? ||||reg||1
Z||000021||batboy||03-11-2000||01:40 AM||||The proper spring rate for you depends on numerous factors, like what type of driving you do and for what purpose. If you are planning on doing some SCCA racing, obviously you'd want stiffer. If this is a daily driver that you just want to improve the handling a little, then slightly softer springs are in order. You actually may have to try a couple different spring rates before you're happy.||||reg||1
Z||000022||batboy||03-11-2000||08:38 AM||||<B>NOTE:</B> I had posted a pic of this earlier, but it was crappy. I got a better pic now, so I deleted the previous post. Maybe you can see what I had done in this one.<P>I took LowCG's advice and had a steel plate welded to the underside of my rear control <BR>arms, partially boxing it in to reduce the chance of flexing.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/gardengoddessl/controlarm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||FIEROCIOUS||03-11-2000||11:23 PM||||I race high speed autocross and SCCA events, and YES it is my daily driver. I don't mind a stiff ride, I just don't wanna lose any teeth! Ryan Motorsports sez 300, Shelby Cutoms sez 350, 325 a happy medium? PLEASE someone help! PLEASE! ||||reg||1
Z||000024||batboy||03-11-2000||11:37 PM||||If you're SCCA racing, then 300# springs <I>might</I> be a tad light, and 350# springs <I>might</I> be a tad stiff for street use. The 325# springs <I>might</I> be a good compromise.||||reg||1
Z||000025||FIEROCIOUS||03-12-2000||06:51 PM||||They MIGHT be?<BR>Thanks batboy LOL.||||reg||12
Z||000026||batboy||03-12-2000||07:39 PM||||Well...your mileage may vary...||||reg||1
Z||000027||FIEROCIOUS||03-13-2000||11:56 AM||||Ok, Let me tell you all my wants and needs for adjustable coilovers. <BR>1. They need to be adjustable.<BR>2. They need to be 2" diameter.<BR>3. They need a collar to sit in or around.<BR>4. When jacked up they need to remain in their perch.<BR>5. They need to have the proper spring rate.<P>Is this too much to ask?<P>Now I did some real measureing of the front coils, so if you ever wondered what the Eibachs spring rates are, they are 260lb/in springs.<BR>How did I get this number? Well I did the spring formula that I posted before, and then heres the fun stuff I first rounded off the number of coils and said 5 active coils but they are actualy 5.2 active coils, now they sit at an agle and will be less effective, at 10 degrees they are 98% effective so take 266 minus 10%.<BR>Approx 260lb/in! That was fun!<P>Can't anyone design springs long enogh to remain in the perch when jacked up? I mean take a longer spring with a lower rate a X amount of compression then install it when the car compresses it lower than what the spring is measured at it will have a higher rate!?????<BR>Help Please I'm going insain!||||reg||1
Z||000028||FIEROCIOUS||03-15-2000||01:18 AM||||batboy, can you be a dear, and post pics with the coil overs installed?<BR>LOL! I crack myself up!? <BR>I'm tired, goin to bed now.||||reg||12
Z||000029||batboy||03-18-2000||01:10 PM||||First of the dear Batmobile rear coil-over pics. New RCC coil-over springs in the rear, poly bushings, new ball joints and tie rod ends, and a bump steer correction kit from RCC. On the right side of the pic is the special water pump pulley and cogged belt setup used on my V8 Fiero. I have a 7/8" rear sway bar, but haven't installed it yet.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/rearsuspension.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||FIEROCIOUS||03-19-2000||01:16 PM||||You da man! So, do they sit in their perch?||||reg||14
Z||000031||batboy||03-19-2000||04:39 PM||||I haven't put tires on it and let it down off the jack stands yet. The weather turned crummy again.||||reg||1
Z||000032||VISCERAL||03-20-2000||07:16 PM||||Hey Batboy, nice. BTW are the rear tie rods the same as the front?||||reg||1
Z||000033||batboy||03-20-2000||09:23 PM||||The outer rear tie rods look similar to the front ones, but are a different part number and cost a lot more than the front tie rod ends. Not sure exactly what the difference is or why they cost more.<P>Ok, I put the rear wheels on and let the jack it down. Decided it wasn't low enough yet. Adjusted it until the coil-overs were in about the lowest possible position. Much better (see pic). Those are 235-60-14 tires in the rear right now. I plan on going to a 255-50-16 which will be a bit taller, so I may have to adjust it back up.<P><B>YES!</B> The springs rattle around somewhat in the low setting with the car jacked up. I don't see anyway around it without going to shorter strut cartridges. I also don't foresee any problems with the way it is. With my RCC coil-overs, everything goes right back into place when I let the jack down. I raised and lowered it several times adjusting it and testing it. No problems! However, I guess I don't plan on doing any "Dukes of Hazard" jumps in a ralley race.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/batlowered.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000034||batboy||03-20-2000||10:02 PM||||Here is a "before" pic, look at the front end compared to the "after" pic!<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/battail2.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||FIEROCIOUS||03-21-2000||10:46 PM||||Look'in GOOD! I ordered Ryanes kit. They have a perch that goes in the middle of the spring so this should center it! <P>Hey read my topic "just ordered Ryanemotorsports spring" Or something like that? <P>I swear no matter what car I am currently working on, no matter how many people own them and modify them I ALWAYS find stuff no one has ever done! This is not a problem till you need help! <P>I'm cursed I tell ya!  ||||reg||1
